## Proof

We determine the boundary $\partial \mathbb{Q}^n$ of $\mathbb{Q}^n$ as a subset of $\mathbb{R}^n$ equipped with the standard (Euclidean) topology.

Recall that for any subset $A$ of a topological space,
$$\partial A = \overline{A}\setminus \operatorname{int}(A) = \overline{A}\cap \overline{A^c},$$
where $\overline{A}$ is the closure and $\operatorname{int}(A)$ is the interior of $A$.

We compute the two ingredients for $A = \mathbb{Q}^n$.

**Step 1: $\overline{\mathbb{Q}^n} = \mathbb{R}^n$.**

It is a classical fact that $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$: every nonempty open interval $(a,b)\subset\mathbb{R}$ contains a rational number. We show $\mathbb{Q}^n$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}^n$.

Let $x=(x_1,\dots,x_n)\in\mathbb{R}^n$ and let $U$ be any open neighborhood of $x$. By definition of the standard topology, $U$ contains an open ball $B(x,\varepsilon)$ for some $\varepsilon>0$, and in turn $B(x,\varepsilon)$ contains the open cube
$$C = (x_1-\delta, x_1+\delta)\times\cdots\times(x_n-\delta, x_n+\delta)$$
for $\delta = \varepsilon/\sqrt{n}>0$ (since the cube of half-side $\delta$ is contained in the ball of radius $\varepsilon$ because any point in the cube is at distance at most $\sqrt{n}\,\delta = \varepsilon$ from $x$).

By density of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$, each interval $(x_i-\delta, x_i+\delta)$ contains some $q_i\in\mathbb{Q}$. Then $q=(q_1,\dots,q_n)\in\mathbb{Q}^n\cap C\subset\mathbb{Q}^n\cap B(x,\varepsilon)\subset\mathbb{Q}^n\cap U$.

Thus every nonempty open set in $\mathbb{R}^n$ meets $\mathbb{Q}^n$, which is exactly the statement $\overline{\mathbb{Q}^n}=\mathbb{R}^n$.

**Step 2: $\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{Q}^n) = \emptyset$.**

We show $\mathbb{Q}^n$ contains no nonempty open set. Let $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$ be any nonempty open set; we show $U\not\subseteq\mathbb{Q}^n$.

Pick any $x\in U$ and $\varepsilon>0$ with $B(x,\varepsilon)\subseteq U$. The complement $\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q}$ (the irrationals) is also dense in $\mathbb{R}$: every nonempty open interval contains an irrational number (for instance, if $a<b$, then $a+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{k}\,(b-a)\to a$ and for large enough $k$ this lies in $(a,b)$ and is irrational). Repeating the cube argument of Step 1 with irrational coordinates, there exists $y=(y_1,\dots,y_n)\in(\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q})^n\cap B(x,\varepsilon)\subseteq U$. In particular $y\in U$ but $y\notin\mathbb{Q}^n$.

So no nonempty open set is contained in $\mathbb{Q}^n$, i.e. $\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{Q}^n)=\emptyset$.

**Step 3: The boundary.**

Combining the two steps,
$$\partial\mathbb{Q}^n = \overline{\mathbb{Q}^n}\setminus\operatorname{int}(\mathbb{Q}^n) = \mathbb{R}^n\setminus\emptyset = \mathbb{R}^n.$$

Equivalently, using the symmetric form $\partial A = \overline{A}\cap\overline{A^c}$: we have $\overline{\mathbb{Q}^n}=\mathbb{R}^n$ (Step 1) and $\overline{(\mathbb{Q}^n)^c}=\overline{(\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q})^n}=\mathbb{R}^n$ by the same argument with the irrationals (which are dense in $\mathbb{R}$, hence $(\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q})^n$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}^n$). Thus
$$\partial\mathbb{Q}^n = \mathbb{R}^n\cap\mathbb{R}^n = \mathbb{R}^n.$$

**Conclusion.** The boundary of $\mathbb{Q}^n$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$ (standard topology) is all of $\mathbb{R}^n$:
$$\boxed{\partial\mathbb{Q}^n = \mathbb{R}^n.}$$

This reflects the fact that $\mathbb{Q}^n$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}^n$ yet has empty interior—every point of $\mathbb{R}^n$, rational or irrational, is a boundary point because every neighborhood of it contains both points of $\mathbb{Q}^n$ and points of its complement.

